    67→\section{Background}
    68→\label{sec:background}
    69→
    70→We first briefly review the necessary definitions and methods, then state the main motivation of this paper in remark~\ref{rem:stiffness-challenges}. 
    71→
    72→\subsection{The Chemical Master Equation}
    73→
    74→\begin{definition}[Chemical Master Equation (CME)]
    75→The \textit{Chemical Master Equation (CME)} describes the time evolution of the probability distribution $p(\boldsymbol{x}, t)$ over the state $\boldsymbol{x}$. For a single state $\boldsymbol{x}$, the CME is
    76→\begin{equation}
    77→    \frac{d}{dt} p(\boldsymbol{x}, t) =
    78→    \sum_{k=1}^{M} \big[\alpha_k(\boldsymbol{x} - \boldsymbol{\nu}_k)\, p(\boldsymbol{x} - \boldsymbol{\nu}_k, t)
    79→    - \alpha_k(\boldsymbol{x})\, p(\boldsymbol{x}, t)\big],
    80→\end{equation}
    81→where $\alpha_k(\boldsymbol{x})$ is the propensity for reaction $k$ at state $\boldsymbol{x}$ and $\boldsymbol{\nu}_k$ is its stoichiometric change vector.
    82→
    83→For the entire state space $\mathbf{X}$, the CME can be written in matrix form as
    84→\begin{equation}
    85→    \frac{d}{dt}\, \boldsymbol{p}(\mathbf{X}, t) = \mathbf{A}\, \boldsymbol{p}(\mathbf{X}, t),
    86→\end{equation}
    87→where $\boldsymbol{p}(\mathbf{X}, t)$ is the probability vector over $\mathbf{X}$ and the \textit{generator matrix} $\mathbf{A}$ has entries
    88→\begin{align}
    89→    \mathbf{A}_{ij} =
    90→    \begin{cases}
    91→        -\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{M} \alpha_k(\mathbf{X}_j), & \text{if } i=j, \\
    92→         \alpha_k(\mathbf{X}_j) & \text{if }\, \mathbf{X}_i=\mathbf{X}_j+\boldsymbol{\nu}_k, \\
    93→    \end{cases}
    94→\end{align}
    95→and $\mathbf{A}_{ij}=0$ when no reaction connects $\mathbf{X}_j$ to $\mathbf{X}_i$.
    96→\end{definition}
    97→
    98→\begin{remark}[Generator matrix properties]
    99→The generator $\mathbf{A}$ satisfies:  
   100→\begin{enumerate}
   101→    \item $\mathbf{A}_{ij}\ge 0$ for $i\neq j$ (off-diagonal nonnegative),
   102→    \item $\mathbf{A}_{ii}\le 0$ (diagonal nonpositive),
   103→    \item $\sum_{i} \mathbf{A}_{ij}=0$ for all $j$ (each column sums to zero),
   104→\end{enumerate}
   105→\end{remark}
   106→
   107→\subsection{Finite State Projection Method}
   108→
   109→\begin{definition}[Finite State Projection]
   110→The \textit{Finite State Projection (FSP)} method approximates the CME by restricting dynamics to a finite subset $\mathbf{X}_J\subset\mathbf{X}$. If $\mathbf{A}_J$ is the sub-generator on $\mathbf{X}_J$ and $\boldsymbol{p}_J(0)$ is the initial distribution restricted to $\mathbf{X}_J$, then
   111→\begin{equation}
   112→    \boldsymbol{p}(\mathbf{X}_J, t) \approx e^{\mathbf{A}_J t}\, \boldsymbol{p}_J(0).
   113→\end{equation}
   114→\end{definition}
   115→
   116→\begin{theorem}[Classical FSP bounds \cite{Munsky2006}]
   117→\label{thm:classical_fsp}
   118→Let $A$ be a CME generator (\,$A_{ij}\ge 0$ for $i\neq j$, $A_{ii}\le 0$, and $\mathbf{1}^\top A=0^\top$\,), and let $A_J$ be the principal submatrix on a finite set $J\subset \mathbf{X}$. Denote by $p_J(t)$ the full CME solution restricted to $J$ and by $e^{A_J t}p_J(0)$ the truncated solution that ignores transitions to $J'$.
   119→
   120→If for some $t_f\ge 0$ and $\varepsilon>0$,
   121→\begin{equation}
   122→\mathbf{1}^\top e^{A_J t_f}\,p_J(0) \;\ge\; 1-\varepsilon,
   123→\label{eq:fsp-mass-cond}
   124→\end{equation}
   125→then the following elementwise bounds hold:
   126→\begin{equation}
   127→e^{A_J t_f}\,p_J(0) \;\le\; p_J(t_f) \;\le\; e^{A_J t_f}\,p_J(0) + \varepsilon\,\mathbf{1}.
   128→\label{eq:fsp-elementwise}
   129→\end{equation}
   130→\end{theorem}
   131→
   132→\subsubsection{Time-stepping and sliding window approaches}
   133→
   134→\begin{figure}[!htbp]
   135→    \centering
   136→    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{lv_grid.pdf}
   137→    \caption{Visualization of the adaptive FSP procedure (species $U$ and $V$) during a single time step in Algorithm~\ref{alg:fsp-naive}. The top row shows the truncation boundary during the expand, evolve, and prune phases, while the bottom row shows the corresponding probability contours.}
   138→    \label{fig:adapt-FSP}
   139→\end{figure}
   140→
   141→For simulations over long time intervals, maintaining a single fixed projection space is generally impractical: the reachable state space grows combinatorially in time, quickly making a static truncation too large to store or inefficient to evolve. A natural approach is to partition the time domain $[0,T]$ into subintervals $[t_k,t_{k+1}]$ and solve a sequence of restricted CME problems, each posed on a projection space $\mathbf{X}_J^{(k)}$ tailored to the states that are relevant during that subinterval \cite{Wolf2010, Dinh_2020}.  
   142→At each time step, the method initializes $\boldsymbol{p}_J^{(k)}(t_k)$ from the previous solution, expands $\mathbf{X}_J^{(k)}$ to include newly reachable states, evolves the distribution forward in time, and prunes states whose probabilities fall below a chosen tolerance.
   143→
   144→\begin{algorithm}[H]
   145→\caption{FSP with Time Stepping and State Space Control}
   146→\label{alg:fsp-naive}
   147→\begin{algorithmic}[1]
   148→\REQUIRE initial state $x_0$, time range $[t_0,t_f]$, propensities $\{\alpha_k(x)\}$, stoichiometries $\{\nu_k\}$, step $\Delta t$
   149→\STATE $t \gets t_0$, \; $J \gets \{x_0\}$, \; $\boldsymbol{p} \gets [1]$
   150→\WHILE{$t < t_f$}
   151→  \STATE \textbf{Expand:} add neighbors of $J$ reachable by one (or preset $r$) reactions
   152→  \STATE \textbf{Assemble:} build $\mathbf{A}_{JJ}$ on $J$
   153→  \STATE \textbf{Evolve:} $\boldsymbol{p} \gets \exp(\mathbf{A}_{JJ}\Delta t)\,\boldsymbol{p}$
   154→  \STATE \textbf{Prune:} remove states with $p(x)$ below a heuristic threshold
   155→  \STATE $t \gets \min\{t+\Delta t,\,t_f\}$
   156→\ENDWHILE
   157→\STATE \textbf{return} $(J,\boldsymbol{p})$ at $t_f$
   158→\end{algorithmic}
   159→\end{algorithm}
   160→
   161→This \emph{sliding-window} approach provides a general template for adaptive FSP methods with dynamically evolving state spaces \cite{Dinh2016}. Many variants differ in how expansion and pruning are performed, but they all follow the same broad framework which is to enlarge the state space to capture new probability flow, evolve the distribution on the truncated generator, and prune regions that have become irrelevant. 
   162→The remainder of this paper focuses on refining and analyzing the pruning and time step determination steps within this general adaptive FSP skeleton.
   163→
   164→
   165→\begin{remark}[Stiffness and multiscale challenges in adaptive FSP]
   166→\label{rem:stiffness-challenges}
   167→Adaptive FSP methods must grapple with stiffness that arises when the generator $\mathbf{A}$ exhibits a wide separation of timescales across the state space, a characteristic of many biochemical reaction networks. This stiffness manifests in two coupled ways. First, \textit{spatial stiffness} emerges through connectivity bottlenecks. As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:bottleneck}, a bridge state $s_b$ with negligible probability but large exit rate $w(s_b) := \sum_{k=1}^M \alpha_k(s_b)$ (defined formally in Definition~\ref{def:flux}) creates a fast pathway between slow metastable regions, and pruning $s_b$ based on probability alone severs this connection, rendering the generator reducible and trapping probability mass in isolated components. Second, stiffness also manifests itself temporally. When probability rapidly redistributes during transient phases, the total flux $\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}(t) = \sum_{\boldsymbol{x}} p(\boldsymbol{x},t) w(\boldsymbol{x})$ becomes large and requires small steps $\Delta t$ for stability, while metastable slow periods with low flux permit large steps for efficiency (Figure~\ref{fig:temporal-stiffness}). These manifestations are coupled through the exit rates $w(\boldsymbol{x})$, which simultaneously create spatial bottlenecks and drive temporal flux variations. Our objective is to design pruning and time-stepping heuristics that jointly address both challenges, preserving network connectivity through flux-aware state selection while adapting $\Delta t$ to track the instantaneous activity level $\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}(t)$, thereby maintaining both structural integrity and computational tractability for robust multiscale simulation.
