   247→\section{Methodology: Flux-Based Adaptive FSP}
   248→\label{sec:methodology}
   249→
   250→This section introduces a state space adaptation approach that preserves network connectivity through flux-aware truncation. We first define and characterize probability flux as a connectivity measure, argue that naive probability-based pruning can destroy essential pathways, then construct an algorithm that maintains structural integrity of the state-space graph while controlling computational cost.
   251→
   252→\begin{definition}[Outgoing probability flux]
   253→\label{def:flux}
   254→Let $w(\boldsymbol{x}) := \sum_{k=1}^M \alpha_k(\boldsymbol{x}) = -\mathbf{A}_{\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{x}}$ denote the total exit rate from state $\boldsymbol{x}$. The \textit{outgoing probability flux} from state $\boldsymbol{x}$ at time $t$ is
   255→\begin{equation}
   256→\Phi(\boldsymbol{x},t) := p(\boldsymbol{x},t) \cdot w(\boldsymbol{x}).
   257→\end{equation}
   258→\end{definition}
   259→
   260→The flux $\Phi(\boldsymbol{x},t)$ measures the instantaneous rate at which probability mass exits state $\boldsymbol{x}$. For small $\Delta t > 0$, approximately $\Phi(\boldsymbol{x},t) \cdot \Delta t$ units of probability mass leave $\boldsymbol{x}$ during the interval $[t, t+\Delta t]$. Crucially, states with large exit rates $w(\boldsymbol{x})$ but low probabilities $p(\boldsymbol{x},t)$ can maintain significant flux, acting as critical connections for probability flow between regions of state space despite being rarely occupied.
   261→
   262→\begin{definition}[Total system flux]
   263→The total probability flux of the system at time $t$ over state set $J$ is
   264→\begin{equation}
   265→\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}(J,t) := \sum_{\boldsymbol{x} \in J} \Phi(\boldsymbol{x},t) = \sum_{\boldsymbol{x} \in J} p(\boldsymbol{x},t) \cdot w(\boldsymbol{x}).
   266→\end{equation}
   267→\end{definition}
   268→

