   777→Substituting the adaptive step $\Delta t_n = \varepsilon_{\Delta t}/\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}$ gives the depth in terms of the \emph{spatial stiffness ratio} $\kappa_n := \lambda_{\max}(J_n,t_n)/\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}(J_n,t_n) \geq 1$:
   778→\begin{equation}
   779→\label{eq:expansion-depth-stiffness}
   780→d_{\min} = \Bigl\lceil \kappa_n\,\varepsilon_{\Delta t} + z_\varepsilon\,\sqrt{\kappa_n\,\varepsilon_{\Delta t}} \Bigr\rceil.
   781→\end{equation}
   782→\end{corollary}
   783→
   784→\begin{proof}
   785→After expanding $J_n$ by depth $d$, every state in $J'$ lies at graph distance $\geq d+1$ from any state in $J_n$.
   786→Since probability mass in $J'$ is zero at time $t_n$ (all mass resides in $J_n$), the outflux $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}}$ is driven entirely by mass that has diffused from the interior of $J_n$ to its boundary during $[t_n, t_n+s]$.
   787→Because each reaction fires at rate $\leq \lambda_{\max}$, the number of reactions along any path in time $s$ is stochastically dominated by $\mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda_{\max}\,s)$.
   788→The probability mass reaching graph distance $d$ from $J_n$ (hence capable of flowing into $J'$) by time $t_n + s$ is therefore at most $\mathrm{P}(\mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda_{\max}\,s) \geq d)$.
   789→Hence $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}}(t_n+s) \leq \lambda_{\max} \cdot \mathrm{P}(\mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda_{\max}\,s) \geq d)$, and integrating using the monotonicity of the Poisson survivor in $s$:
   790→\[
   791→\int_{t_n}^{t_{n+1}} \Phi_{\mathrm{out}}(s)\,ds
   792→\;\leq\;
   793→\lambda_{\max}\,\Delta t_n \cdot \mathrm{P}\!\bigl(\mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda_{\max}\,\Delta t_n) \geq d\bigr).
   794→\]
   795→Setting $\mu = \lambda_{\max}\,\Delta t_n$ and applying the CLT approximation
   796→$\mathrm{P}(\mathrm{Poisson}(\mu) \geq d) \approx 1 - \Phi((d-\mu)/\sqrt{\mu})$,
   797→requiring $\mu \cdot \mathrm{P}(\mathrm{Poisson}(\mu) \geq d) \leq \varepsilon_{\mathrm{exp}}$ yields~\eqref{eq:expansion-depth}.
   798→Substituting $\Delta t_n = \varepsilon_{\Delta t}/\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}$ gives $\mu = \kappa_n\,\varepsilon_{\Delta t}$ and hence~\eqref{eq:expansion-depth-stiffness}.
   799→\end{proof}
   800→
   801→\begin{remark}[Expansion strategy and the depth-one regime]
   802→\label{rem:expansion-strategy}
   803→Corollary~\ref{cor:expansion-depth} shows that depth $d=1$ suffices whenever $\mu_n\leq 1$.  This is achieved by the max-propensity cap (Remark~\ref{rem:max-propensity-cap}) unconditionally, or by the maximum-flux rule when $\epsilon_{\Delta t}\leq\Phi_{\max}/\lambda_{\max}$.  Within this depth-one regime two practical expansion strategies are available:
   804→\begin{itemize}
   805→  \item \textbf{Stoichiometric expansion} adds every state reachable by a single reaction step from any active state, covering all $R$ reaction channels simultaneously.  This is the conservative choice, appropriate when $\kappa_n>1$ (multiple fast channels) or when the dominant reaction direction is not known \textit{a priori}.
   806→  \item \textbf{SSA expansion} samples one next-reaction direction per active state according to the propensity distribution, adding only the most probable single-step neighbor.  When $\kappa_n\approx 1$ (a single reaction channel dominates locally), SSA implements $d=1$ with substantially lower state-space growth: stoichiometric expansion inflates $|S_n|$ by up to factor $R$ per step, while SSA adds exactly one new state per active state.
   807→\end{itemize}
   808→In systems where $\kappa_n$ varies across the state space---one dominant channel locally but multiple channels relevant globally---stoichiometric expansion offers safety without requiring prior knowledge of which channel will dominate.
   809→\end{remark}
   810→
   811→\begin{remark}[Consistent parameter selection via spatial stiffness]
   812→\label{rem:consistent-params}
   813→Corollaries~\ref{cor:local-error-pruning}, \ref{cor:flux-timestep}, and~\ref{cor:expansion-depth} and Remarks~\ref{rem:max-propensity-cap} and~\ref{rem:expansion-strategy} provide a self-consistent framework for parameter selection, governed by the spatial stiffness ratio $\kappa=\lambda_{\max}/\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}$:
   814→\begin{itemize}
   815→  \item \textbf{Time step} (three options in increasing aggressiveness):
   816→    total-flux $\Delta t = \varepsilon_{\Delta t}/\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}$ (leading-order error $\lesssim\varepsilon_{\Delta t}$, $\mu=\kappa\,\varepsilon_{\Delta t}$; \textbf{recommended});
   817→    max-propensity cap $\Delta t = \min(\varepsilon_{\Delta t}/\Phi_{\mathrm{total}},\,1/\lambda_{\max})$ (error $\lesssim\min(\varepsilon_{\Delta t},1/\kappa)$, always $\mu\leq 1$);
   818→    maximum-flux $\Delta t = \varepsilon_{\Delta t}/\Phi_{\max}$ (error $\lesssim\eta\,\varepsilon_{\Delta t}$, $\mu\leq 1$ when $\varepsilon_{\Delta t}\leq\Phi_{\max}/\lambda_{\max}$; valid only when $\eta_n\approx 1$, otherwise risks solution collapse for spread distributions)
   819→    \hfill (Corollary~\ref{cor:flux-timestep}, Remark~\ref{rem:max-propensity-cap}),
   820→  \item \textbf{Expansion depth and strategy:} use $d_{\min}=\lceil\kappa\,\varepsilon_{\Delta t}\rceil$ with stoichiometric expansion under the total-flux rule; reduce to $d_{\min}=1$ via the cap or maximum-flux rule, choosing SSA expansion when $\kappa\approx 1$ (one dominant channel) and stoichiometric expansion when $\kappa>1$ \hfill (Corollary~\ref{cor:expansion-depth}, Remark~\ref{rem:expansion-strategy}),
   821→  \item \textbf{Flux tolerance:} $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{flux}} \ll \varepsilon_{\Delta t}$, e.g., $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{flux}} = \varepsilon_{\Delta t}/w_{\max}$ \hfill (Corollary~\ref{cor:local-error-pruning}).
   822→\end{itemize}
   823→In the bottleneck example of Section~\ref{sec:results}, $\kappa\approx k_2/k_1=10^5$ early in the simulation: the total-flux rule alone would require $d_{\min}\approx 10^5$, but the max-propensity cap enforces $\Delta t\leq 1/\lambda_{\max}=1/k_2$, reducing $d_{\min}$ to 1 while delivering local error $1/\kappa\ll\varepsilon_{\Delta t}$.
   824→The maximum-flux rule is advantageous for spatially stiff systems with concentrated distributions ($\kappa\gg 1$, $\eta\approx 1$): it takes larger steps than the total-flux rule while automatically maintaining $d=1$ when $\varepsilon_{\Delta t}\leq\Phi_{\max}/\lambda_{\max}$.
   825→
   826→\end{remark}
